<div class="sec-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">The shape</p>
  <h2>A file manager that runs your ssh.</h2>
  <p>pālana has no connection stack of its own. It spawns the system
  <code>ssh</code> binary, so your config, keys, agent, and
  <code>ProxyJump</code> behave exactly as they do in your terminal, and it
  holds one ControlMaster session per host so everything after the first
  connection runs with near-zero overhead. Hosts come from
  <code>~/.ssh/config</code>: if <code>ssh koan</code> lands you on koan,
  koan is already in the menu. Transfers are <code>rsync -a -s --partial
  --info=progress2</code>, a tar stream when one end lacks rsync, and
  <code>zfs send | zfs receive</code> for whole datasets. Nothing goes on
  the servers.</p>
</div>

<div class="frow">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <h3>The shell is one key away.</h3>
    <p><kbd>⌘`</kbd> opens your actual ssh on the focused host in the panel
    under the panes—one warm session per machine. The footer shows who has
    the keyboard; <kbd>⌘`</kbd> gives it back. Do the tedious part by hand,
    then return to the panes. For a quick question without a shell,
    <kbd>⇧tab</kbd> arms four read keys: <kbd>d</kbd> <kbd>z</kbd>
    <kbd>s</kbd> <kbd>p</kbd> drop <code>df</code>, <code>zfs list</code>,
    <code>zpool status</code>, and <code>zpool list</code> into the
    panel.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="frow-visual">
    <figure class="plan">
      <div class="plan-panel">
        <div class="pp-head">
          <span class="pp-phase">shell · jodo</span>
          <span class="pp-enter">⌘` hands the keyboard back</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="pp-body">tyro@jodo:/tank/media/camera$ zfs list -o name,used,avail -r tank/media
NAME                 USED  AVAIL
tank/media          79.4G  1.21T
tank/media/camera   79.4G  1.21T
tyro@jodo:/tank/media/camera$ █</pre>
      </div>
      <figcaption>shell has the keyboard · ⌘` returns it to the panes</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="frow rev">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <h3>Every plan is a command you could have typed.</h3>
    <p>Press <kbd>y</kbd> or <kbd>m</kbd> and the panel shows what will run,
    on which host, by which route. <kbd>⏎</kbd> runs it with live output.
    <kbd>esc</kbd> closes it. Or select the command line and paste it into
    the shell below, or into a terminal on another machine, or into your
    notes. pālana uses the same <code>ssh</code> and <code>rsync</code> you
    do, so the command works anywhere you can reach the host.
    <a href="/the-plan/">Read the plan model →</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="frow-visual">
    <figure class="plan">
      <div class="plan-panel">
        <div class="pp-head">
          <span class="pp-phase">move · the plan</span>
          <span class="pp-enter">⏎ enter runs</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="pp-body"><strong>move · move to another machine</strong>
<span class="faint">1 entry · 41.3 GB</span>
<span class="faint">jodo:/tank/media/camera → koan:/rpool/cold</span>
<span class="faint">how: rsync, run host-to-host · using the forwarded ssh key</span>

$ rsync -a -s --partial --info=progress2 /tank/media/camera/archive-2025 koan:/rpool/cold/
  runs only after the copy above is verified: $ rm -rf /tank/media/camera/archive-2025</pre>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sec-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">The plan</p>
  <h2>The command is on screen before it runs.</h2>
</div>

<div class="frow">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <p>Press <kbd>m</kbd> and nothing moves. The panel opens with the
    operation, the route, and the exact command. A move across dataset
    boundaries is a copy followed by a verified delete; the plan says so,
    and the <code>rm</code> line waits until <code>find | wc -l</code>
    agrees on both ends—two commands you watch run. <kbd>⏎</kbd> runs the
    plan. <kbd>esc</kbd> leaves it alone.</p>
    <p>If you already know rsync, the plan is a receipt. If you are
    learning, it is a worked example on your own hosts and paths, ready to
    copy. <a href="/the-plan/">Read the plan model →</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="frow-visual">
    <figure class="plan">
      <div class="plan-panel">
        <div class="pp-head">
          <span class="pp-phase">move · the plan</span>
          <span class="pp-enter">⏎ enter runs</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="pp-body"><strong>move · copy then delete the original</strong>
<span class="faint">1 entry · 41.3 GB</span>
<span class="faint">jodo:/tank/media/camera → jodo:/rust/archive</span>
<span class="faint">how: runs locally on the one host involved</span>

$ rsync -a -s --partial --info=progress2 /tank/media/camera/archive-2025 /rust/archive/
  runs only after the copy above is verified: $ rm -rf /tank/media/camera/archive-2025</pre>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The command, its route, and the condition for deletion.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sec-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">ZFS</p>
  <h2>Datasets, snapshots, and send/receive as file-manager verbs.</h2>
</div>

<div class="frow rev">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <p>A filled drive mark on a folder is a dataset mountpoint, so pālana
    knows whether a move is a rename or a 41&nbsp;GB copy and says which in
    the plan. Press <kbd>Z</kbd> and the pane becomes the host's dataset
    tree: create, snapshot, roll back, destroy, mount, unmount, set the
    mountpoint. Move a whole dataset between two ZFS machines and the plan
    upgrades itself to <code>zfs snapshot</code>, then <code>zfs send -R |
    ssh | zfs receive -u</code>—block-level, host to host, cleanup after
    the receive is verified. Destroy asks you to type the word. The pool
    root is off limits. Hosts without ZFS get everything else.
    <a href="/zfs/">ZFS, natively →</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="frow-visual">
    <figure class="plan">
      <div class="plan-panel">
        <div class="pp-head">
          <span class="pp-phase">move · the plan</span>
          <span class="pp-enter">⏎ enter runs</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="pp-body"><strong>move · move to another machine</strong>
<span class="faint">how: zfs send/receive, run host-to-host · using the forwarded ssh key</span>

$ zfs snapshot -r tank/sage/jodo/kanyo@palana-20260716-101530
$ zfs send -R -v tank/sage/jodo/kanyo@palana-20260716-101530 | ssh koan 'zfs receive -u rpool/sage/koan/kanyo'
  runs only after the copy above is verified: $ zfs destroy -r rpool/sage/koan/kanyo@palana-20260716-101530
  runs only after the copy above is verified: $ zfs destroy -r tank/sage/jodo/kanyo</pre>
      </div>
      <figcaption>A snapshot, one stream, and cleanup after verification.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sec-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">Routing</p>
  <h2>Your Mac gives the order. The servers move the bytes.</h2>
</div>

<div class="frow">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <p>For a copy between two servers, pālana forwards your agent so jodo
    authenticates to koan directly. Your key stays on your Mac. The
    41&nbsp;GB goes host to host instead of across your Wi-Fi twice. The
    plan's <code>how:</code> line names the route it chose—host to host,
    through your Mac because your Mac is one end, or through your Mac as
    the fallback—and the transport it picked.
    <a href="/moving-files/#cross-host">Cross-host moves →</a></p>
  </div>
