  <div class="bleed-inner">
    <h2>A small key grammar.</h2>
    <p>Arrows work. So do the vim keys. Six lowercase verbs, a few prefixes,
    and consistent rules cover the rest of the keyboard.</p>
    <div class="keys-row">
      <span><kbd>j</kbd> <kbd>k</kbd> <kbd>h</kbd> <kbd>l</kbd> move</span>
      <span><kbd>y</kbd> copy</span>
      <span><kbd>m</kbd> move</span>
      <span><kbd>d</kbd> delete</span>
      <span><kbd>/</kbd> jump</span>
      <span><kbd>f</kbd> the field</span>
      <span><kbd>?</kbd> all keys</span>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/keys/">Read the key map →</a></p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sec-head">
  <h2>The rest of the tool.</h2>
</div>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">⌥ drop</span><h3>Drag and drop, with a plan</h3><p>Between panes, from Finder, or onto a folder row. A drop composes a plan like any verb. Hold ⌥ to make it a move.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">⏎ save</span><h3>Edit remote files in your own editor</h3><p>Open a server's config in your Mac app; saving offers to send it back. If the file changed remotely, it asks first.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">v</span><h3>Preview follows selection</h3><p>The right pane tracks the cursor in the left: text, images, PDFs. Remote reads are bounded.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">*</span><h3>Favorites, per host or global</h3><p>Favorites belong to a host by default. Promote one to global and it appears in every host menu.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">⌘,</span><h3>Add hosts without editing config</h3><p>The form shows the exact block it will write to ~/.ssh/config, after backing the file up.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">⌘+ ⌘−</span><h3>One master zoom</h3><p>The whole surface scales together, 0.8× to 1.6×. Light and dark, both warm.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">⇧⌘L</span><h3>A plain-text log</h3><p>Every executed operation appends to a plain-text file in Application Support you can grep.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="tag">esc</span><h3>Errors that name the fix</h3><p>When something cannot happen, pālana states the reason and usually puts the fix beside it.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="bleed darkband">
  <div class="bleed-inner">
    <h2>No background process.</h2>
    <p>pālana runs when you open it and stops when you close it. No daemon,
    no launch agent, no polling, no telemetry. There is no component that
    <em>could</em> outlive the window.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      <p><strong class="accent">Your ssh, verbatim.</strong> No embedded SSH stack. The system binary, your config, your keys.</p>
      <p><strong class="accent">The command, verbatim.</strong> What the plan shows is what runs, byte for byte.</p>
      <p><strong class="accent">Deletion after verification.</strong> The destructive half of a move runs only after visible count checks agree on both ends.</p>
      <p><strong class="accent">State in plain text.</strong> Five files in Application Support—topology cache, session, settings, favorites, an append-only log. No database. Delete the cache whenever; the hosts are the system of record.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sec-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">Implementation</p>
  <h2>Built in the open.</h2>
</div>

<div class="frow">
  <div class="frow-text">
    <p>Swift 6, strict concurrency, SwiftPM. The logic lives in
    <strong>PalanaCore</strong>, a headless library above 97% test coverage.
    The app renders state and forwards intent; it cannot compose a shell
    command on its own. All host contact goes through the system
    <code>ssh</code> binary via <code>Process</code>, one ControlMaster
    session per host. The design record is public in the repo's
    <code>ho-process/</code>: each architectural decision, its reasoning,
    and the code it scoped.
    <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works →</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="frow-visual">
    <div class="codeblock">$ git clone https://github.com/sageframe-no-kaji/palana.git
$ cd palana &amp;&amp; swift build
<span class="c"># swift test runs the whole battery—the core holds a 90% coverage floor</span></div>
  </div>
</div>

